{"vid":"bRnGm3y0IFU","source":"bRnGm3y0IFU.en-GB.json3","lang":"en","cues":[{"t":0.4,"txt":"Ice – that's this. But what phrases can we use with 'ice'?"},{"t":10.6,"txt":"If you 'break the ice', you do or say something to make the atmosphere less tense."},{"t":17.6,"txt":"It could be tense because everyone is strangers or because of something like an argument."},{"t":23.7,"txt":"I know you two still aren't talking. Shall we go for a coffee to break the ice?"},{"t":29.1,"txt":"A game that breaks the ice is called an 'icebreaker'."},{"t":33,"txt":"These are often used at the start of courses to help everyone get to know each other."},{"t":38.6,"txt":"If you're 'skating on thin ice',"},{"t":40.6,"txt":"you're doing something risky which could have negative consequences."},{"t":45.1,"txt":"You're skating on thin ice by not doing your homework. She's going to be so mad with you."},{"t":51.3,"txt":"If you 'put a plan on ice', you decide to delay the plan."},{"t":55.8,"txt":"We were going to go ice skating,"},{"t":57.8,"txt":"but we've put the plan on ice because my sister's broken her leg!"},{"t":67.3,"txt":"This is 'a stone', but what are some useful phrases to use with it?"},{"t":75.4,"txt":"A plan or a decision can be 'set in stone'."},{"t":78.8,"txt":"That means 'you can't change it' – it's fixed."},{"t":82.4,"txt":"Sorry… the essay deadline is set in stone. No exceptions!"},{"t":87.9,"txt":"If you 'kill two birds with one stone', you achieve two things at once."},{"t":93.4,"txt":"I listen to podcasts while I'm commuting to work – it kills two birds with one stone."},{"t":98.9,"txt":"If you 'leave no stone unturned', it means you look everywhere"},{"t":103.3,"txt":"until you find something or try"},{"t":106.2,"txt":"absolutely everything until you've achieved something."},{"t":110.1,"txt":"The detectives promised to leave no stone unturned in their search for a suspect."},{"t":115.8,"txt":"If something is 'a stone's throw away', it's a very short distance away – it's close!"},{"t":122.1,"txt":"My new flat is just a stone's throw away from work – I can walk there!"},{"t":126.8,"txt":"Try to incorporate these phrases into your everyday life!"},{"t":136.2,"txt":"This is my face, but let's learn some phrases with 'face'."},{"t":144.8,"txt":"If you struggle to 'keep a straight face', it's hard to stay serious."},{"t":150.2,"txt":"We usually use this about situations where you need to not laugh, but you can't help it."},{"t":156.7,"txt":"I couldn't keep a straight face – it was so funny!"},{"t":161,"txt":"When you've done something wrong and have to deal with the consequences,"},{"t":164.7,"txt":"you can say you have to 'face the music' – 'deal with the issue'."},{"t":169.4,"txt":"Oh no… time to face the music. Wish me luck."},{"t":174.3,"txt":"If something is 'in your face', it's bold and brave…"},{"t":179.4,"txt":"Like wearing that to work."},{"t":181.6,"txt":"Wow, that is quite in your face!"},{"t":185,"txt":"If something is 'written all over your face', you can't hide your emotions."},{"t":190.7,"txt":"I can tell you don't like my new haircut – it's written all over your face!"},{"t":201,"txt":"What's the saddest colour?"},{"t":203.6,"txt":"Some people say it's blue, but are there any phrases we can use with 'blue'?"},{"t":212.5,"txt":"Let's stay with sadness."},{"t":214.6,"txt":"If you've 'got the blues', then it just means 'you're feeling sad'."},{"t":219.5,"txt":"Don't worry – everyone gets the blues sometimes."},{"t":222.8,"txt":"Just leave me alone – I've got a bad case of the blues."},{"t":226.4,"txt":"We've done sadness. Now, let's think about anger."},{"t":230.7,"txt":"If someone is screaming until they're 'blue in the face',"},{"t":234,"txt":"they're angry but they're wasting their time."},{"t":237.3,"txt":"We use this phrase with people who are unhappy and complaining a lot."},{"t":241.7,"txt":"It means their complaints won't help."},{"t":244.4,"txt":"You can scream until you're blue in the face, but I'm not going to change your grade."},{"t":248.5,"txt":"Sadness and anger are negative."},{"t":251.3,"txt":"Hopefully they only affect you 'once in a blue moon'."},{"t":254.9,"txt":"A blue moon is quite rare, so we use it to talk about things that almost never happen."},{"t":260.7,"txt":"If I oversleep, I'll be late. Luckily that only happens once in a blue moon."},{"t":265.9,"txt":"So, we've seen three phrases with 'blue'. Now, try to use them this week!"},{"t":276.5,"txt":"You know what the sea is. But do you know any phrases with 'sea'?"},{"t":285.5,"txt":"If you're 'at sea', you feel confused and maybe a bit lost."},{"t":290.6,"txt":"I studied so hard… I don't know how I failed – I'm completely at sea."},{"t":296.6,"txt":"If your relationship has ended or someone you like is no longer single,"},{"t":301.7,"txt":"you might say there are 'plenty more fish in the sea'"},{"t":304.7,"txt":"– 'there are lots of other people you could have a relationship with'."},{"t":309.5,"txt":"I know it's hard breaking up, but there are plenty more fish in the sea."},{"t":314.5,"txt":"If you look out at 'a sea of something',"},{"t":317.4,"txt":"it means 'you can see lots of things in front of you'."},{"t":320.8,"txt":"For example, when it rains, London turns into a sea of umbrellas!"},{"t":326.3,"txt":"And if you 'go to sea', you set out on a voyage."},{"t":330.1,"txt":"You can't go to sea in this weather – there's a huge storm coming!"},{"t":340.3,"txt":"This is my ear, but what phrases can we make with 'ear'?"},{"t":348.9,"txt":"If something is 'music to your ears', it's great news that makes you happy."},{"t":354.2,"txt":"We're getting a pay rise? That's music to my ears!"},{"t":358.1,"txt":"If you don't make a plan, you can 'play it by ear'"},{"t":360.8,"txt":"– 'just see what happens and be spontaneous'."},{"t":364.7,"txt":"We don't have a schedule for the trip – we're just going to play it by ear."},{"t":369.4,"txt":"If you 'lend an ear', you listen sympathetically to someone who needs support."},{"t":375.5,"txt":"You look upset. Do you want me to lend an ear?"},{"t":379.1,"txt":"And finally, if you're 'up to your ears in something',"},{"t":382.5,"txt":"it's taking all your time and attention"},{"t":384.8,"txt":"– you're extremely busy and at capacity."},{"t":388.4,"txt":"I can't go out tonight – I'm up to my ears in work!"},{"t":392.6,"txt":"Now, go out there and use these phrases,"},{"t":395.3,"txt":"and don't forget to lend an ear to your friends."},{"t":404,"txt":"Fire is hot and dangerous. So, what kind of things can we express with the word 'fire'?"},{"t":413.6,"txt":"If you 'play with fire', you're doing something risky or dangerous."},{"t":417.9,"txt":"Leaving your car unlocked in the city?"},{"t":420.1,"txt":"If you play with fire, you're going to get burnt."},{"t":423.5,"txt":"If you describe someone as 'on fire', it means 'they're performing amazingly'."},{"t":429.4,"txt":"Did you see Molly's presentation? She's on fire today!"},{"t":432.8,"txt":"She's doing so well."},{"t":435.4,"txt":"But if you're 'under fire', that means 'you're being criticised or attacked'."},{"t":440,"txt":"This comes from fire referring to bullets."},{"t":443.6,"txt":"Bill was under fire because he kept turning up late to work."},{"t":448.3,"txt":"What happens if you 'add fuel to the fire'? You make it bigger!"},{"t":452.8,"txt":"You make a bad situation even worse."},{"t":456.7,"txt":"Don't argue with her now – you'll only add fuel to the fire."},{"t":461.1,"txt":"Practise these phrases and you'll be on fire in your English conversations!"},{"t":471.3,"txt":"Here's my hat. It's better than yours!"},{"t":474.3,"txt":"Let's learn three phrases with 'hat'."},{"t":479.8,"txt":"I'm keeping something 'under my hat'. No, it's not my hair – it's a secret."},{"t":485.9,"txt":"If someone asks you to 'keep something under your hat', then they mean 'don't tell anyone else'."},{"t":492.5,"txt":"We're planning a surprise party for Georgie, but keep it under your hat."},{"t":497.4,"txt":"Next, we're going to talk about wearing many hats."},{"t":501.4,"txt":"Usually, it's talking about jobs you do, or the kind of tasks you do in those jobs."},{"t":507,"txt":"If you 'wear many hats', then you do different jobs or different tasks in your job."},{"t":512.9,"txt":"You'll need lots of skills because you have to wear many hats in this job."},{"t":517.2,"txt":"Enough about wearing a hat – now, let's take one off!"},{"t":521.6,"txt":"If you 'take your hat off to someone', you recognise something good that they've done."},{"t":527.4,"txt":"You don't actually need a hat – you just say,"},{"t":530.2,"txt":"\"I take my hat off to you.\""},{"t":532,"txt":"Thanks for this amazing day. I have to take my hat off to everyone involved in organising it – you've done so well!"},{"t":538.8,"txt":"You don't need a hat – you just need these phrases with 'hat'."},{"t":543.4,"txt":"Try to use them this week!"},{"t":550.9,"txt":"We know the basic meaning of head, but what phrases do you know with 'head'?"},{"t":559.6,"txt":"If you 'give someone heads up', you tell them something in advance."},{"t":564.4,"txt":"It could be some helpful information or a warning."},{"t":568.4,"txt":"You'll receive a very important email later. I'm just giving you a heads up."},{"t":574.3,"txt":"If you're 'head over heels in love', you are completely in love."},{"t":579.6,"txt":"We often use this to describe the beginning of a relationship."},{"t":584.4,"txt":"Oh, look at them! They're head over heels in love."},{"t":588.6,"txt":"If you 'lose your head', you are overcome with emotion."},{"t":593.4,"txt":"Your presentation is really good. Don't lose your head – stay calm."},{"t":599.4,"txt":"And if you 'say something off the top of your head',"},{"t":602.7,"txt":"you say it without thinking much before speaking."},{"t":606.3,"txt":"This is usually because you don't have enough time."},{"t":610.2,"txt":"How many people are coming? Hmm… I'd say 200, off the top of my head."},{"t":620.9,"txt":"I'm sure you know what a heart is, but do you know these phrases with 'heart'?"},{"t":630.2,"txt":"They're all really useful, so maybe you should 'learn them by heart'."},{"t":635.4,"txt":"That means 'memorise them so you can remember them without notes'."},{"t":639.3,"txt":"For the test, I need you to learn these formulas by heart."},{"t":643.4,"txt":"If you can't remember them, you'll fail."},{"t":645.8,"txt":"Of course, I know you're great at learning formulas. Eat your heart out, Einstein!"},{"t":652.1,"txt":"We say 'eat your heart out' to mean you think you are even better at something"},{"t":657.1,"txt":"than someone famous for doing it, though it's often just a joke."},{"t":660.6,"txt":"Look at how I play football – eat your heart out, Ronaldo!"},{"t":664.2,"txt":"We often use 'heart' to talk about feelings."},{"t":667.3,"txt":"If you're someone who shows their feelings very clearly,"},{"t":670.6,"txt":"we can say that you 'wear your heart on your sleeve'."},{"t":674.4,"txt":"If he was unhappy, you'd know about it – he always wears his heart on his sleeve."},{"t":679.8,"txt":"So, here are three phrases with 'heart'."},{"t":683.4,"txt":"Try to learn them by heart and use them this week!"}],"words":[{"t":0.4,"w":"Ice"},{"t":0.4,"w":"–"},{"t":0.4,"w":"that's"},{"t":0.4,"w":"this."},{"t":0.4,"w":"But"},{"t":0.4,"w":"what"},{"t":0.4,"w":"phrases"},{"t":0.4,"w":"can"},{"t":0.4,"w":"we"},{"t":0.4,"w":"use"},{"t":0.4,"w":"with"},{"t":0.4,"w":"'ice'?"},{"t":10.64,"w":"If"},{"t":10.64,"w":"you"},{"t":10.64,"w":"'break"},{"t":10.64,"w":"the"},{"t":10.64,"w":"ice',"},{"t":10.64,"w":"you"},{"t":10.64,"w":"do"},{"t":10.64,"w":"or"},{"t":10.64,"w":"say"},{"t":10.64,"w":"something"},{"t":10.64,"w":"to"},{"t":10.64,"w":"make"},{"t":10.64,"w":"the"},{"t":10.64,"w":"atmosphere"},{"t":10.64,"w":"less"},{"t":10.64,"w":"tense."},{"t":17.6,"w":"It"},{"t":17.6,"w":"could"},{"t":17.6,"w":"be"},{"t":17.6,"w":"tense"},{"t":17.6,"w":"because"},{"t":17.6,"w":"everyone"},{"t":17.6,"w":"is"},